The embedding writers name their files themselves: `write_recording_embeddings` takes the label of the data embedded (e.g. `label="base"`) and adds `Random<seed>` for random weights and the pooling's name for window vectors pooled otherwise than by the mean, and `write_transformed_embeddings` takes the fitted transform (`transform=aligner`) and adds its `label` (`EaCoralCh`, `Ra`, ...) to the label of its source. The rule is public as `coco_pipe.io.embedding_label`, so a caller can find the files (`find_embeddings(root, embedding_label("base", seed=3), "token")`) without rebuilding the names. Each subject aligner has a `label`. `write_transformed_embeddings` no longer takes `label`, and `transform` is the aligner instead of a mapping. It refuses a per-channel transform (`channel_axis`) whose axis is not the source's `channel` axis, as for tokens with one token per time patch (LUNA).
